Q: Best practices for architecting a Go CLI with ~100 commands and many flags. How can I organize commands/flags? A: <p>I have long followed the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Command_pattern" rel="nofollow noreferrer">command pattern</a> for this, and a sprinkle of a Chain-of-Responsibility. Let me explain:</p>
<p>A CLI is a <em>natural</em> fit for the command pattern, wherein you can capture the function representing a command in a dispatch table. Upon looking up the command, you strip off the first argument (since that is the command) and pass the remaining arguments to the underlying function for parsing/processing. You can repeat this indefinitely for subcommand.</p>
<p>The beauty of this approach is that you can simply add functions to handle any new commands making it very easy to maintain. This is a super-performant approach since map lookup of a string is <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Big_O_notation" rel="nofollow noreferrer">O(1)</a>.</p>
<pre class="lang-golang prettyprint-override"><code>package main

import (
    &quot;fmt&quot;
    &quot;os&quot;
    &quot;sort&quot;
    &quot;strings&quot;
)


func main() {
    if len(os.Args) &lt; 2 {
        fmt.Println(&quot;Usage: gocli &lt;command&gt; [args...]&quot;)
        return
    }

    cmdName := os.Args[1]
    args := os.Args[1:]

    if cmd, ok := Commands[cmdName]; ok {
        result, err := cmd(args...)
        if err != nil {
            fmt.Printf(&quot;Error: %v\n&quot;, err)
            os.Exit(1)
        }

        fmt.Println(result)
    } else {
        fmt.Printf(&quot;Command %s not found\n&quot;, cmdName)
        os.Exit(1)
    }
}

// CommandFunc is a type for our command functions
type CommandFunc func(args ...string) (string, error)

// WrapCommand strips the first argument before calling the original function
func WrapCommand(f CommandFunc) CommandFunc {
    return func(args ...string) (string, error) {
        if len(args) &gt; 0 {
            return f(args[1:]...)
        }
        return f()
    }
}

var Commands map[string]CommandFunc

func init() {
    Commands = map[string]CommandFunc{
        &quot;hello&quot;: func(args ...string) (string, error) {
            name := &quot;World&quot;
            if len(args) &gt; 0 {
                name = args[0]
            }
            return fmt.Sprintf(&quot;Hello, %s!&quot;, name), nil
        },
        &quot;add&quot;: func(args ...string) (string, error) {
            return fmt.Sprintf(&quot;Adding something with args: %v&quot;, args), nil
        },
        &quot;help&quot;: helpCommand,
    }

    for name, cmd := range Commands {
        Commands[name] = WrapCommand(cmd)
    }
}

func helpCommand(_ ...string) (string, error) {
    var keys []string
    for k := range Commands {
        keys = append(keys, k)
    }
    sort.Strings(keys)
    return fmt.Sprintf(&quot;Available commands: %s&quot;, strings.Join(keys, &quot;, &quot;)), nil
}
</code></pre>

Q: How to eagerly initialize an async ASP.NET Core service? A: <p>Code that you have shared</p>
<pre><code>var a = app.Services.GetRequiredService&lt;ServiceA&gt;();
var b = app.Services.GetRequiredService&lt;ServiceB&gt;();
// brittle! Pray the order is correct.
await a.EnsureIndexesAsync(startupCancellation);
await b.InitializeAsync(startupCancellation);
</code></pre>
<p>is valid, if you need to do so. I don't see problem with it.</p>
<p>If you want to ensure that indexes are &quot;ensured&quot; (whatever it means), before invoking <code>ServiceB.InitializeAsync</code>, I would define one more service</p>
<pre><code>public class ServiceC(ServiceA a, ServiceB b)
{
    public async Task EnsureAndInitializeAsync(CancellationToken token)
    {
        await a.EnsureIndexesAsync(startupCancellation);
        await b.InitializeAsync(startupCancellation);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>and then use <code>ServiceC</code>.</p>
<p>Another idea would be, since <code>ServiceB</code> already depends on <code>ServiceA</code>, why don't call <code>EnsureIndexesAsync</code> at the very beginning of <code>ServiceB.InitializeAsync</code>?</p>
<pre><code>public sealed class ServiceB 
{
    // dependency without clear timing requrements, will call a.use() eventually
    public ServiceB(ServiceA a) {...}
    public async Task InitializeAsync(CancellationToken cancellationToken) 
    {
        await a.EnsureIndexesAsync(cancellationToken);
        ...
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: WPF: Drawing on top of a TextBlock A: <p>Your problems are:</p>
<ol>
<li><code>GetVisualChild()</code> should return <code>base.GetVisualChild(index)</code> except when <code>index==base.VisualChildrenCount</code>.</li>
<li>You forgot to call <code>AddVisualChild()</code> when DrawingExtra becomes true or DrawingVisual changes</li>
<li>You forgot to call <code>RemoveVisualChild()</code> when DrawingExtra becomes false or DrawingVisual changes</li>
</ol>
<p>You can fix #2 and #3 by setting a <code>PropertyChangedCallback</code> on DrawingExtra and adding code to the setter of DrawingVisual.</p>
<p>Explanation:  It is the <code>AddVisualChild()</code> call that actually adds the visual to the tree.  What is happening is that your visual is being found and displayed &quot;accidentally&quot; because of your error in <code>GetVisualChild()</code>, but it is not being properly linked into the visual tree so you'll encounter many problems.</p>
<p><strong>Update</strong></p>
<p>I edited your code as described above, and it worked perfectly.  Here are the changes:</p>
<pre><code>...
      {
        PropertyChangedCallback = (obj, e) =&gt;
          {
            var textBlock = (DerivedTextBlock)obj;
            if((bool)e.OldValue) textBlock.RemoveVisualChild(textBlock.DrawingVisual);
            if((bool)e.NewValue) textBlock.AddVisualChild(textBlock.DrawingVisual);
          }
      });

  public DrawingVisual DrawingVisual
  {
    get { return _drawingVisual; }
    set
    {
      if(DrawExtra) RemoveVisualChild(_drawingVisual);
      _drawingVisual = value;
      if(DrawExtra) AddVisualChild(_drawingVisual);
    }
  }
  private DrawingVisual _drawingVisual;

...

  protected override int VisualChildrenCount
  {
    get { return base.VisualChildrenCount + (DrawExtra ? 1 : 0); }
  }

  protected override Visual GetVisualChild(int index)
  {
    return index==base.VisualChildrenCount ? DrawingVisual : base.GetVisualChild(index);
  }
</code></pre>
